 The chrome sizes here are GopDesk's, and `dk-bar-h` and `dk-task-h` have to
 agree by hand because GopDesk cites this one and the dependency cannot run
 the other way. Both are scaled by `ui-scale`, which steps at a different
 width from the `ui-wscale` a widget minimum takes.

 We say:

Section: Geometry

  gsc-topbar-h : Integer = 22
  gsc-taskbar-h : Integer = 28

 Cordic answers on a scale of 1000 and takes milliradians, 6283 to the
 turn. The orbit radius is 5200 world units, so the scaled cosine is
 multiplied by 5.2 to land there.

  gsc-radius : Real = 5.2
  gsc-eye-height : Real = 2200.0
  gsc-turn : Integer = 6283

Section: Scene

  gsc-camera : Integer, Real -> Camera3D
  gsc-camera (angle) (aspect) =
    let ca = real-from-int (cordic-cos angle)
    in let sa = real-from-int (cordic-sin angle)
    in let c = camera3d-new (vec3-new (ca * gsc-radius) gsc-eye-height (sa * gsc-radius)) (vec3-new 0.0 300.0 0.0) 0.785
    in __record-set c "c3-aspect" aspect

 `camera3d-new` takes no aspect and its constructor answers the literal 1.333
 (`Scene3D.codex:64`), which `mat4-perspective` divides X by. Every caller in
 the tree accepted that, so the scene was correct only where the viewport
 happened to be 4:3 and stretched everywhere else in proportion. The aspect
 belongs to the VIEWPORT, so it is supplied here rather than defaulted there;
 setting the field leaves the other eighteen callers and their goldens alone.

  gsc-aspect : Integer, Integer -> Real
  gsc-aspect (w) (h) = if h <= 0 then 1.333 else real-from-int w / real-from-int h

 The ground texture is named rather than inlined because two places have to
 agree on it exactly: the scene the software path samples, and the single
 upload gsc-run hands the rasterizer.

  gsc-ground-tex : EngineTexture
  gsc-ground-tex = etx-checkerboard 8 8 1 (etx-pack-rgb 170 150 120) (etx-pack-rgb 110 95 75)

 Every primitive here takes a HALF-extent, so a placement is the centre and the
 body reaches half the size in each direction. The cube sits at 900 rather than
 450 for that reason: mesh-cube 900 is 1800 across, and at 450 it stood 450
 units THROUGH the floor. The ball and the ring are pushed out along z far
 enough to clear the cube's 1800-unit box, the ball by 300 and the ring by 700,
 which are the smallest moves that separate them. All three had intersected
 since the scene was written; a flat untextured ground and a broken shadow pass
 are what kept it from being visible.

  gsc-scene : Integer -> Scene3DState
  gsc-scene (dummy) =
    let scene = scene3d-new (gsc-camera 0 1.333)
    in let checker = gsc-ground-tex
    in let ground = sn3-with-mesh "ground" (mesh-plane 3000 3000) (emat-set-texture (emat-flat (rgb 240 240 240)) checker)
    in let cube = sn3-set-pos (sn3-with-mesh "cube" (mesh-cube 900) (emat-shiny (rgb 90 130 210) 32)) (-900.0) 900.0 0.0
    in let pyr = sn3-set-pos (sn3-with-mesh "pyr" (mesh-pyramid 600 1100) emat-red) 1100.0 0.0 (-300.0)
    in let ball = sn3-set-pos (sn3-with-mesh "ball" (mesh-sphere 700 12 8) emat-gold) 300.0 700.0 1700.0
    in let ring = sn3-set-pos (sn3-with-mesh "ring" (mesh-torus 650 220 12 8) (emat-set-shading (emat-color (rgb 60 180 90)) GouraudShaded)) (-2000.0) 220.0 1900.0
    in let s2 = scene3d-add (scene3d-add (scene3d-add (scene3d-add (scene3d-add scene ground) cube) pyr) ball) ring
    in let key = dir-light (vec3-new (-400.0) 800.0 400.0) (rgb 255 250 235) 850
    in scene3d-set-ambient (scene3d-add-light s2 key) 260

Section: Loop

  gsc-blit-span : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gsc-blit-span (dst) (doff) (src) (soff) (w) (x) =
    if x >= w then 0
    else let d = poke-32 dst ((doff + x) * 4) (peek-32 src ((soff + x) * 4))
    in gsc-blit-span dst doff src soff w (x + 1)

 THE SOURCE HAS A STRIDE OF ITS OWN, and it is what lets a window resize
 without allocating. The colour buffer is allocated once at the content box
 and a smaller window renders the top-left `w` by `h` of it, so the source
 row pitch is the BUFFER's width and not the copy's. It was `y * w` and that
 was correct only while every render filled the whole buffer.

  gsc-blit-rows : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gsc-blit-rows (dst) (stride) (src) (sstride) (w) (h) (y) =
    if y >= h then 0
    else let d = gsc-blit-span dst (y * stride) src (y * sstride) w 0
    in gsc-blit-rows dst stride src sstride w h (y + 1)

 The shadow map target is rebuilt by r3d-render-shadowed every frame,
 inside the loop's bracket, so its half megabyte is reclaimed with the
 rest of the frame.

  gsc-shadow-map : Integer = 256

 The host rasterizer fills its map in C, so it can afford a far finer one
 than the software pass, which builds its 256 in Codex a triangle at a time.
 The map covers the whole scene bounds, and the ground is 3000 units across,
 so at 256 a single texel lands on many screen pixels and every shadow edge
 arrives as texel-shaped teeth.

  gsc-shadow-map-gpu : Integer = 1024

 Which path starts is the caller's to say, and the desk says the host
 rasterizer when the machine has one and software when it does not. Either
 stays reachable through G on a box with the rasterizer, which is what makes
 a parity comparison possible at all.

  gsc-label-sw : Text = "3D: software pipeline. S toggles shadows, G the GPU. F12 shoots. Esc returns."

  gsc-label-gpu : Text = "3D: host rasterizer, culled, shadow-mapped. S toggles shadows, G software."

  gsc-frame : R3dTriState, Scene3DState, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, Integer, Integer, GpuView -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory] Integer
  gsc-frame (tgt) (scene) (angle) (sky) (base) (stride) (font) (ch) (sh) (gpu) (gv) = act
    let posed = scene3d-set-camera scene (gsc-camera angle (if gpu == 1 then gsc-aspect (gv.gv-w) (gv.gv-h) else gsc-aspect (tgt.r3t-w) (tgt.r3t-h)))
    in if gpu == 1 then act
      g <- if sh == 1 then gs-render-shadowed posed gv sky gsc-shadow-map-gpu else gs-render-in posed gv sky
      let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride (gv.gv-x) (ch - gsc-label-band) (tgt.r3t-w) gsc-label-band sky
      in gop-draw-text base stride font (gv.gv-x + 8) (ch - 16) gsc-label-gpu 65280 1 0
    end
    else
      let c = r3d-target-clear tgt sky
      in let d = if sh == 1 then r3d-render-shadowed tgt posed gsc-shadow-map else r3d-render-into tgt posed
      in let b = gsc-blit-rows (base + gv.gv-x * 4) stride (tgt.r3t-base) (tgt.r3t-stride) (tgt.r3t-w) (tgt.r3t-h) 0
      in gop-draw-text base stride font (gv.gv-x + 8) (ch - 16) gsc-label-sw 65280 1 0
  end

 The frame is bracketed because there is no collector and this loop has
 no bound: left open, an unbracketed frame's heap is gone for good, and
 the shadow pass adds a half-megabyte map rebuild to every frame on top
 of the geometry temporaries. Everything the bracket drops is built
 inside the frame -- the posed camera, the Cordic reals, the shadow map,
 the render's temporaries. The target and the scene are built once in
 gsc-run, below the mark, so the restore cannot reach them, and the
 angle comes from the RTC rather than an accumulator, so no iteration
 reads what the last one built. The RTC and not the PIT tick cell,
 because the ASUS kills its timer interrupt about 54 seconds after
 boot and never restarts it -- the board fact that froze the desk
 clock on 2026-08-04 froze this orbit on the first A6 flight.

 The mark sits ABOVE the keyboard poll rather than below it, so the
 bracket covers the whole iteration and not just the render. GopUsbKbd
 argues its idle path allocates nothing, and reading it that is true
 today; but on a board with a USB keyboard the pump runs a three-phase
 state machine on every frame, and the cost of this loop should not
 depend on a claim about another chapter that nothing re-checks. Only
 the scancode has to cross the restore, and an Integer is not heap.
 What the pump writes survives it either way: the ring, the report and
 the phase cell are fields built before the loop, and the mailbox at
 gk-cell is a fixed address.

 The shot writes the live frame to the boot stick and paints the
 verdict into the taskbar band, which this loop never repaints, so the
 verdict survives the next frame. Colours are GopDesk's taskbar pair,
 hand-synced like the chrome sizes above.

  gsc-shot : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont -> [Device.Port] Integer
  gsc-shot (scr-base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) = act
    let mark = __heap-save
    in act
      t <- shot-take scr-base w h stride
      let s = ui-scale w
      in let bg = gop-fill-rect scr-base stride (200 * s) (h - gsc-taskbar-h * s + 4 * s) (300 * s) (16 * s) #000E1C
      in let tx = gop-draw-text scr-base stride font (204 * s) (h - gsc-taskbar-h * s + 6 * s) t #69BE28 s 0
      in let d = __heap-restore mark
      in 0
    end
  end

 `kbd-pump-one` advances a THREE-phase state machine (arm, check, drain)
 by one step per call and `kbd-take` calls it once, so a loop gets one
 step per iteration. Every other pane iterates far faster than its
 keyboard and never notices. This one costs about a second a frame on
 metal, which made one poll per frame a report every three seconds, and
 only if the key was still held when the sample landed. Measured on the
 ASUS 2026-08-08: keys were swallowed unless held down and Esc would not
 close the pane. Polling until the machine yields costs microseconds
 against a second-long frame.

  gsc-poll-steps : Integer = 12

  gsc-poll : UsbKbd, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  gsc-poll (kbd) (n) = act
    if n <= 0 then 0
    else act
      sc <- kbd-take kbd
      if sc /= 0 then sc
      else gsc-poll kbd (n - 1)
    end
  end

  gsc-angle : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gsc-angle (now) (mins) = int-mod (((if now < 0 then 0 else now) + (gsc-bcd mins) * 60) * 105) gsc-turn

 `n` counts frames RENDERED, not iterations: the shot arm recurses without
 touching it, so a run spent pressing keys does not inflate the rate. It also
 advances when the picture does not, which is the whole reason it is a
 separate reading from anything the frame draws -- under a pinned clock the
 orbit angle is constant and every frame is identical, and the counter is then
 the only thing on the glass that moves.

 The S and G arms RESET it, because each changes what a frame costs. Left
 running, a measurement taken after a toggle is an average over both settings
 weighted by how long the run sat in each, which is a number about the
 operator rather than about the renderer.

Section: Step

 The loop moved to `GopDesk` on 2026-08-18. Both 3D panes shared it, so one
 step serves both: the scene BUILDER stays a parameter, as it was on the loop,
 and the desk passes `gsc-scene` or `gf-scene` according to which pane has
 focus. It cannot become a field on the record instead, because `GopFish` cites
 this chapter and a discriminator resolved here would have to call back into it.

 `ScenePane` is everything the loop carried that is not derivable: the render
 target, the viewport, the two toggles and the frame counter's pair. The
 toggles and the counter are integers stored in place, so the desk's
 per-iteration restore is harmless to them and this pane never has to keep a
 frame. The target is built once in `gsc-new`, below that mark, exactly as it
 was built below the loop's.

 **Mouse-look, since 2026-08-19, and the reason it is a TAKEOVER rather than an
 addition.** The camera angle came from `gsc-orbit`, which is driven by the HPET
 and never stops. Adding a mouse offset to a moving angle gives a view that
 drifts out from under the hand, so the first pointer movement latches
 `sp-look` and the orbit stops being consulted from then on: the scene turns
 itself until you touch it, and after that you turn it. There is no way back to
 the orbit short of closing the pane, which is the honest cost of one bit.

 `sp-ptr` is the last pointer position this pane acted on, packed as
 `x * 4096 + y`, and ZERO means never sampled: without that the first frame
 reads a delta against an origin the pointer was never at and the view snaps.
 The three fields are integers stored in place, so the desk's per-iteration
 restore is harmless to them, which is the same reason the toggles are.

 THE TWO BUFFERS ARE THE PANE'S AND THE TARGET IS THE FRAME'S. `sp-px` and
 `sp-dp` are allocated once, at the size of the whole content box, which is
 the largest rectangle a window on this desk can take. `sp-tgt` is rebuilt
 from them whenever the window moves or resizes, with `r3t-w` and `r3t-h` the
 window's and `r3t-stride` the buffer's. **That is what makes maximise and
 restore cost nothing and strand nothing**: a target reallocated per resize
 would leave the old one above the pane's own heap mark until the pane
 closed, which is the LIFO tail 6.4 measured, and about 4 MB of a 128 MB
 arena each time somebody pressed the button.

 The depth buffer is indexed `y * r3t-w + x` rather than by the stride, so a
 smaller window packs it differently from a larger one. Nothing carries
 across a frame: `r3d-target-clear` rewrites every word of it before the
 render, so the packing is the frame's own business.

  ScenePane = record {
    sp-tgt : R3dTriState,
    sp-px : Integer,
    sp-dp : Integer,
    sp-bw : Integer,
    sp-gv : GpuView,
    sp-sky : Integer,
    sp-ch : Integer,
    sp-fb : Integer,
    sp-sh : Integer,
    sp-gpu : Integer,
    sp-n : Integer,
    sp-t0 : Integer,
    sp-yaw : Integer,
    sp-look : Integer,
    sp-ptr : Integer
  }

 The gain is milliradians of yaw per device pixel, and `gsc-turn` is 6283, so a
 full turn is 785 pixels of travel at 8. The delta is clamped before it is used
 rather than after: an unclamped jump plus one `gsc-turn` can still land
 negative, and `int-mod` of a negative is not the wrap this wants.

  gsc-look-gain : Integer = 8
  gsc-look-max : Integer = 64

  gsc-look-clamp : Integer -> Integer
  gsc-look-clamp (d) = if d > gsc-look-max then gsc-look-max else if d < 0 - gsc-look-max then 0 - gsc-look-max else d

 IT TAKES THE BOX AND NOT THE SCREEN. The caller decides where this pane
 lives, because a window can be anywhere and the chrome constants this
 chapter used to derive it from (`gsc-topbar-h`, `gsc-taskbar-h`, and a
 sidebar edge) were a hand-synced copy of the desk's own. `gsc-place` is
 what puts it somewhere; `gsc-new` only says how big the buffers are.

  gsc-new : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory] ScenePane
  gsc-new (base) (stride) (cs) (gpu) (sky) (bw) (bh) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let px = alloc-bytes (bw * bh * 4)
    in let dp = alloc-bytes (bw * bh * 4)
    in act
      tx <- if gpu == 1 then gs-tex-upload gsc-ground-tex else 0
      let started = hpet-start
      in ScenePane {
        sp-tgt = R3dTriState { r3t-base = px, r3t-depth = dp, r3t-w = bw, r3t-h = bh, r3t-stride = bw },
        sp-px = px,
        sp-dp = dp,
        sp-bw = bw,
        sp-gv = gv-new 0 0 bw (bh - gsc-label-band),
        sp-sky = sky,
        sp-ch = bh,
        sp-fb = base,
        sp-sh = 1,
        sp-gpu = gpu,
        sp-n = 0,
        sp-t0 = hpet-ticks,
        sp-yaw = 0,
        sp-look = 0,
        sp-ptr = 0
      }
    end
  end

 The step drains the keyboard itself and that is not redundant with the desk's
 own take. `kbd-pump-one` advances a THREE-phase state machine by one step per
 call, so one take per frame is one PHASE per frame, and a frame here costs
 about a second on metal. Measured on the ASUS 2026-08-08, before `gsc-poll`
 existed: keys were swallowed unless held down and Esc would not close the
 pane. `desk-loop` takes once per iteration and an iteration is a frame, so
 without this the defect comes straight back. The desk's scancode is used when
 it found one and the poll runs only when it did not.

 WHERE THE PANE IS, SET BEFORE EVERY FRAME RATHER THAN ONCE. All four fields
 are stored in place, so the desk's per-iteration heap restore is harmless to
 them for the same reason the toggles are, and a window that moved between
 two frames is simply drawn at its new place by the next one.

 `y` is the top of the pane's CONTENT, under the titlebar. `sp-fb` carries
 the row offset and `sp-gv`'s x carries the column, because that is the split
 `gsc-frame` and `gsc-hud` already read: the blit destination is
 `sp-fb + gv-x * 4` and the label band is measured down from `sp-fb` by
 `sp-ch`.

  gsc-place : ScenePane, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gsc-place (sp) (base) (stride) (x) (y) (w) (h) =
    let a = __record-set sp "sp-gv" (gv-new x y w (h - gsc-label-band))
    in let b = __record-set sp "sp-ch" h
    in let c = __record-set sp "sp-fb" (base + y * stride * 4)
    in let d = __record-set sp "sp-tgt" (R3dTriState { r3t-base = sp.sp-px, r3t-depth = sp.sp-dp, r3t-w = w, r3t-h = h, r3t-stride = sp.sp-bw })
    in 0

  gsc-take : UsbKbd, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  gsc-take (kbd) (sc0) = act
    if sc0 /= 0 then sc0
    else gsc-poll kbd (gsc-poll-steps - 1)
  end

 The cursor is hidden before each frame rather than once on entry. `desk-loop`
 draws it at the pointer before any step runs, and a frame that blitted over it
 would leave the save buffer describing pixels that no longer exist, so the
 next pointer move would stamp them back into the scene. Hide and blit in that
 order is the same rule section 5 of the desk contract states; the effect is
 that this pane shows no cursor, which is what it did before.

  gsc-step-hide : Integer = 2

  gsc-look : ScenePane, UsbMouse -> Integer
  gsc-look (sp) (mouse) =
    let px = (mouse-x mouse) * 4096 + (mouse-y mouse)
    in let moved = mouse.um-ok & sp.sp-ptr /= 0 & px /= sp.sp-ptr
    in let d = if moved then gsc-look-clamp ((mouse-x mouse) - (sp.sp-ptr / 4096)) else 0
    in let a = __record-set sp "sp-yaw" (int-mod (sp.sp-yaw + d * gsc-look-gain + gsc-turn) gsc-turn)
    in let b = __record-set sp "sp-ptr" (if mouse.um-ok then px else sp.sp-ptr)
    in let c = __record-set sp "sp-look" (if moved then 1 else sp.sp-look)
    in 0

  gsc-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, (Integer -> Scene3DState), ScenePane, Integer -> [Device.Port, Gpu.Compute, Gpu.Memory] Integer
  gsc-step (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (kbd) (mouse) (cs) (build) (sp) (sc0) = act
    sc <- gsc-take kbd sc0
    if sc == 1 then act
      v <- (gs-viewport-release)
      0
    end
    else if sc == 15 then act
      v <- (gs-viewport-release)
      gsc-step-hide
    end
    else if sc == 88 then act
      s <- (gsc-shot base w h stride font)
      1
    end
    else if sc == 31 then
      let a = __record-set sp "sp-sh" (1 - sp.sp-sh)
      in let b = __record-set sp "sp-n" 0
      in let c = __record-set sp "sp-t0" hpet-ticks
      in 1
    else if sc == 34 then
      let a = __record-set sp "sp-gpu" (1 - sp.sp-gpu)
      in let b = __record-set sp "sp-n" 0
      in let c = __record-set sp "sp-t0" hpet-ticks
      in 1
    else act
      now <- rtc-seconds 0
      mins <- rtc-read 2
      let lk = gsc-look sp mouse
      in let ang = if sp.sp-look == 1 then sp.sp-yaw else gsc-orbit now mins
      in let hd = cursor-hide cs base stride
      in act
        f <- gsc-frame (sp.sp-tgt) (build now) ang (sp.sp-sky) (sp.sp-fb) stride font (sp.sp-ch) (sp.sp-sh) (sp.sp-gpu) (sp.sp-gv)
        let k = __record-set sp "sp-n" (sp.sp-n + 1)
        in let hud = gsc-hud (sp.sp-fb) stride font (sp.sp-ch) (sp.sp-gv) (sp.sp-n) (sp.sp-t0)
        in 1
      end
    end
  end

 Milliseconds per frame rather than frames per second, because it is an
 integer at every rate this renderer reaches and needs no formatting: a
 second a frame reads 1000, sixty a second reads 16. `hpet-ticks` is a
 counter, never waited on, so a board with no HPET and a bed with a pinned
 clock both answer a zero span; that arm prints the count alone rather than
 dividing by it.

  gsc-hud : Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, GpuView, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gsc-hud (base) (stride) (font) (ch) (gv) (n) (t0) =
    let tps = hpet-ticks-per-second
    in let d = hpet-ticks - t0
    in let ms = if tps <= 0 then 0 else d * 1000 / tps
    in let t = if ms <= 0 then "f" & show n else "f" & show n & " " & show (ms / n) & "ms"
    in gop-draw-text base stride font (gv.gv-x + gv.gv-w - text-length t * 8 - 8) (ch - 16) t 65280 1 0

  gsc-bcd : Integer -> Integer
  gsc-bcd (n) = (n / 16) * 10 + int-mod n 16

 The orbit runs off the HPET counter, not the RTC seconds register. Both give
 105 milliradians a second and a turn in just under a minute, but the RTC has a
 one-second granularity, so the pane rendered 43 identical frames and then
 jumped six degrees: measured, 17,945 pixels of a 250x170 patch changed between
 one RTC second and the next. That reads as an animation running at a frame a
 second no matter how fast the renderer is.

 The HPET counter is read, never waited on, so the ASUS killing its timer
 INTERRUPT about 54 seconds after boot does not reach this. A board with no
 HPET answers a zero period, and a halted counter answers zero ticks; the RTC
 expression is still there for both.

 `hpet-start` is called here rather than once at entry because the counter is
 HALTED until something sets the enable bit, and a halted counter reads a
 constant -- which does not look like a missing clock, it looks like an
 animation that has stopped. E1000e starts it at the point of use for the same
 reason. It answers the existing config when the bit is already set, so the
 repeat costs two MMIO reads.

  gsc-orbit : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gsc-orbit (now) (mins) =
    let started = hpet-start
    in let tps = hpet-ticks-per-second
    in let ticks = hpet-ticks
    in if tps <= 0 then gsc-angle now mins
    else if ticks <= 0 then gsc-angle now mins
    else int-mod (ticks * 105 / tps) gsc-turn

 The viewport is released on the way out rather than left armed. Nothing
 else on the desk reaches the rasterizer today, so a stale rect would not
 be visible until something did, which is the kind of debt that surfaces
 as somebody else's bug.

 The view stops short of the label row. The host rasterizer clears its
 whole viewport and draws a frame far faster than the glass is sampled,
 so a label inside the rect reads as absent about as often as present.
 The software path does not have the problem because a frame there costs
 about a second.

  gsc-label-band : Integer = 20

 The texture uploads ONCE here, not in the frame. It is the same checker every
 iteration, and the commit costs a host-side allocation, a copy out of guest RAM
 and a line of host diagnostic output per call: driven from gsc-frame it fired
 301 times in a nine-second capture, which is invisible headless and turns the
 interactive pane into about a frame a second with a visible flicker.

  gsc-sky : Integer = rgb-to-packed (rgb 20 20 30)

Page 1
